---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "BC PNP Draw November 13, 2026: 384+ Invitations for Childcare, Health Care, Construction and Vet Workers";
const description = "BC PNP draw November 13, 2026: 384+ invitations for ECEs, health care, construction and vet workers, with minimum scores from 72 to 100. What changed and why.";
const canonical = "https://bcpnpcalculator.ca/articles/bc-pnp-draw-november-13-2026";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "When was the latest BC PNP draw?",
    a: "As of November 14, 2026, the latest BC PNP invitation round was held on November 13, 2026. It was a Skills Immigration targeted round for four Care and Build categories: childcare (early childhood educators only), health care, veterinary care and construction trades. WelcomeBC updated its Invitations to Apply page the same day.",
  },
  {
    q: "What were the minimum scores in the November 13, 2026 BC PNP draw?",
    a: "Childcare needed 95 points (153 invitations), health care 72 (103 invitations), veterinary care 100 (fewer than five invitations) and construction trades 94 (128 invitations). That is 384 invitations plus a veterinary count WelcomeBC reports only as fewer than five.",
  },
  {
    q: "Did BC PNP scores go down in November 2026?",
    a: "For two of the four categories, yes. Compared with the October 8, 2026 round, the childcare cutoff fell from 99 to 95 and the health care cutoff fell from 76 to 72, the lowest in either category since the Care and Build draws began on May 6, 2026. Construction stayed at 94 for a third straight round, and veterinary care rose from 86 to 100.",
  },
  {
    q: "Was the November 13 draw linked to IRCC's extra PNP allocations?",
    a: "There is no official link. IRCC announced on November 12, 2026 that provinces can use optional extra PNP space to move workers already in Canada to permanent residence, but neither WelcomeBC nor the B.C. government had announced B.C.'s share or any change to the BC PNP as of November 14, 2026. The November 13 round followed B.C.'s usual monthly pattern for Care and Build draws.",
  },
  {
    q: "How often does BC PNP hold Care and Build draws?",
    a: "Roughly once a month in 2026. Targeted Care and Build rounds were held on May 6, June 2, July 9, August 6, September 10, October 8 and November 13. B.C. does not announce draw dates in advance, so the only confirmation is an update to the Invitations to Apply page on WelcomeBC.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should I do if I received an invitation on November 13, 2026?",
    a: "You have 30 calendar days from the date of the invitation to submit a complete application through BCPNP Online, so plan for a deadline of around December 13, 2026, and confirm the exact date in your account. The Skills Immigration application fee has been $1,750 since January 22, 2026. Prepare your job offer documents, proof of work experience and any required professional designation before you start.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a },
  })),
};
---

<ArticleLayout slug="bc-pnp-draw-november-13-2026" datePublished="2026-11-14" dateModified="2026-11-14" articleSection="News" keywords="BC PNP draw November 2026, BC PNP draw November 13 2026, latest BC PNP draw, BC PNP childcare draw, BC PNP health care draw, BC PNP construction draw, BC PNP minimum score, BC PNP ECE invitations" ogImage="/images/articles/bc-pnp-draw-november-13-2026.png" title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="inline-block bg-red-100 text-red-800 px-4 py-1 rounded-full text-sm font-bold mb-4 border border-red-200 shadow-sm">
        BC PNP News
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP Draw November 13, 2026: 384+ Invitations for Childcare, Health Care, Construction and Vet Workers
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        B.C.'s seventh Care and Build round of 2026 lowered the cutoffs for early childhood educators and health care workers, held construction steady and raised the bar for veterinary technologists.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-11-14">November 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="mb-10">
      <img src="/images/articles/bc-pnp-draw-november-13-2026.png" width="1200" height="630" alt="Four score cards comparing the BC PNP November 13, 2026 minimum scores for childcare, health care, construction and veterinary care with the October 8 round."
           class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    </figure>

    <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-600 p-6 mb-10 rounded-r-xl">
      <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-2">Quick answer</p>
      <p class="text-red-900">
        On November 13, 2026, the BC PNP invited 384 Skills Immigration candidates, plus fewer than five veterinary candidates, in four targeted categories: early childhood educators (153 invitations, minimum score 95), priority health care occupations (103, minimum 72), construction trades (128, minimum 94) and veterinary care (fewer than five, minimum 100). Childcare and health care cutoffs fell four points each from the October 8 round, reaching their lowest levels since these draws began in May 2026.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">384+</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Invitations issued on November 13, 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">72</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Health care cutoff, lowest since targeted draws began in May</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">7,235</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Skills Immigration registrations in the pool (November 3, 2026)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        British Columbia held a new BC PNP draw on Thursday, November 13, 2026, its first Skills Immigration round since the High Economic Impact invitations of October 29. WelcomeBC posted the results on its <a href="https://www.welcomebc.ca/immigrate-to-b-c/about-the-bc-provincial-nominee-program/invitations-to-apply" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Invitations to Apply page</a>, last updated November 13, 2026. The round targeted the province's Care and Build priorities, which have had their own monthly draws since May. Here is what was announced, how it compares with earlier rounds, and what it means if you are in the pool.
      </p>

      <h2 id="results" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">November 13, 2026 BC PNP Draw Results</h2>

      <p>
        All four categories were Skills Immigration targeted invitations. WelcomeBC notes that the childcare, health care and construction rounds were conducted as described on its "selection of workers" page, meaning candidates had to meet the occupation-specific conditions for each category, not just the score.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 text-base">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Category</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Who was eligible</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Minimum score</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Invitations</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">Care: Childcare</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Early childhood educators only</td><td class="px-4 py-3">95</td><td class="px-4 py-3">153</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">Care: Health</td><td class="px-4 py-3">All priority health care occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72</td><td class="px-4 py-3">103</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">Care: Veterinary care</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Priority veterinary care occupations, including animal health technologists and veterinary technicians (NOC 32104) with a valid professional designation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Fewer than 5</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">Build: Construction trades</td><td class="px-4 py-3">All priority construction occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3">94</td><td class="px-4 py-3">128</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The three published counts add up to 384. Because WelcomeBC reports the veterinary figure only as "fewer than 5," the exact total is not published, but it cannot exceed 388. That is about 100 more invitations than the October 8 round, which issued at least 284, but fewer than the August and September rounds, which each passed 400.
      </p>

      <h2 id="compared" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How the Scores Compare With Earlier 2026 Care and Build Draws</h2>

      <p>
        The table below shows every Care and Build round since the targeted draws started on May 6, 2026, using WelcomeBC's published minimum scores.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 text-base">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Draw date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Childcare</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Health</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Veterinary</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Construction</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t bg-red-50 font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">November 13, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">95</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">94</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">October 8, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76</td><td class="px-4 py-3">86</td><td class="px-4 py-3">94</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">September 10, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76</td><td class="px-4 py-3">90</td><td class="px-4 py-3">94</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">August 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">102</td><td class="px-4 py-3">84</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72</td><td class="px-4 py-3">88</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">July 9, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td><td class="px-4 py-3">96</td><td class="px-4 py-3">88</td><td class="px-4 py-3">97</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">June 2, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">111</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">92</td><td class="px-4 py-3">101</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-3">May 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">115</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Health care: down 36 points since May</h3>
      <p>
        The health care cutoff has fallen in almost every round, from 108 in May to 72 now. A minimum of 72 is within reach for many registrants with a B.C. job offer in a priority health occupation, even without a high wage or a regional job. If you work in health care and have held back from registering because you assumed your score was too low, run your numbers again with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-red-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-nurses-health-care-assistants-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">guide for nurses and health care assistants</a> covers the registration and designation rules that apply to this category.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Childcare: below 99 for the first time</h3>
      <p>
        Early childhood educators needed 95 points, after two rounds stuck at 99. With 153 invitations, this was also one of the larger childcare rounds of the year, behind only August (183) and September (182). The category is limited to early childhood educators; under the Skills Immigration Program Guide, that means holding a valid ECE One Year or ECE Five Year Certificate from B.C.'s ECE Registry. Assistant certificates do not qualify. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-early-childhood-educators-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">ECE guide</a> for the employer conditions as well.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Construction: steady at 94</h3>
      <p>
        Construction trades held at 94 for the third round in a row, with 128 invitations, up from 115 in October. A flat cutoff with more invitations suggests a steady supply of construction registrations at or just above that level. Workers in this category should look at our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-skilled-trades-construction-pathway-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">skilled trades and construction pathway</a> article for the SkilledTradesBC details.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Veterinary care: up to 100</h3>
      <p>
        The veterinary cutoff jumped from 86 to 100. With fewer than five invitations in most rounds, this category is small and the minimum score can swing widely depending on who happens to be in the pool, so a single high result says little about the next round.
      </p>

      <h2 id="pool" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the Pool Looks Like</h2>

      <p>
        WelcomeBC's latest pool snapshot, dated November 3, 2026, shows 7,235 Skills Immigration registrations across all occupations. The largest groups scored 90 to 99 (1,435 registrations), 100 to 109 (1,351) and 80 to 89 (1,234). Only 865 registrations scored 120 or more, and just 15 scored 140 or higher. The snapshot does not break registrations down by occupation, so it cannot tell you how many early childhood educators or health workers are competing in each category. It does show that most of the pool sits in the same 80 to 110 range where the Care and Build cutoffs now fall.
      </p>
      <p>
        WelcomeBC also states that invitations go only to qualified registrations received before the date of the draw. A registration created on or after November 13 had to wait for the next round.
      </p>

      <h2 id="context" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Bigger Picture: B.C.'s 2026 Allocation and the New Federal Space</h2>

      <p>
        The BC PNP's 2026 nomination allocation is 6,254 after the federal government added 1,000 spaces in August, and the program has said it <a href="https://www.welcomebc.ca/immigrate-to-b-c/about-the-bc-provincial-nominee-program/news" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">plans to use its full allocation by December 31, 2026</a>. By our count of WelcomeBC's tables, the seven Care and Build rounds since May have issued more than 2,600 invitations. Not every invitation becomes a nomination, but with about seven weeks left in the year, more rounds are likely before December 31. B.C. does not announce dates in advance.
      </p>
      <p>
        The draw came one day after IRCC announced on November 12, 2026 that provinces and territories can use <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/news/2026/11/minister-metlege-diab-highlights-how-canada-is-working-with-provinces-and-territories-to-support-worker-retention.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">optional extra PNP space</a> to move workers already in Canada to permanent residence. There is no sign the two are connected: the November 13 round fits B.C.'s monthly Care and Build pattern, and as of November 14, 2026 neither WelcomeBC nor the B.C. government had announced B.C.'s share of the extra space or any new stream. We explain the federal measure in <a href="/articles/extra-pnp-nominations-2027-workers-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">extra PNP nominations for workers in Canada</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Federally, Express Entry has still not held a round since October 28, 2026 (round 453), as of November 14. BC PNP nominees in the Express Entry BC category depend on provincial nominee rounds to receive their invitation for permanent residence. Track those on our <a href="/articles/express-entry-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry draws</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2 id="next" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What To Do Now</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>If you were invited:</strong> you have 30 calendar days from the invitation date to submit a complete application through BCPNP Online, which points to a deadline of around December 13, 2026. Check the exact date in your account. The Skills Immigration application fee is $1,750. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-after-ita-next-steps-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">after-ITA guide</a> walks through the documents.</li>
        <li><strong>If you are in a Care or Build occupation and were not invited:</strong> compare your score with the new cutoffs. Points for a professional designation, B.C. work experience or a job outside Metro Vancouver can make the difference next month.</li>
        <li><strong>If you are not in a targeted occupation:</strong> your route is the High Economic Impact round, which last invited candidates on October 29 with a $49 an hour and $99,000 a year wage track or a 131-point cutoff. Historical results are on our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep your registration current:</strong> registrations stay in the pool for up to 12 months. If your wage, job or language test changes, update your profile so your score is accurate before the next round.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This article is general information, not legal advice. Cutoffs change with every round, and past scores do not guarantee future invitations.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">{f.q}</h3>
